# No Backward Compatibility Until the Project Is Open

Until this project is open to external developers, replace an old implementation when a better one exists. Do not keep the old one for backward compatibility.

- Change function signatures when a better one exists.
- Delete superseded code.
- Add a second API for the same purpose only when the two serve genuinely distinct purposes.

Two exceptions, where the original must be preserved:

1. Historical algorithm fidelity. When replicating a published algorithm (for example 1980s yolk and heart algorithms from Miller, Feld, Grofman, and others), keep the original logic and cite the source. Put improvements in separate, clearly named functions.
2. Externally sourced code brought in verbatim. Treat it as read-only unless you have explicit permission to modify it.

When the project opens to other developers, this rule is superseded by semantic versioning and the deprecation policy in `milestone_gates.md` (First binding / 1.0) and ROADMAP.md (Documentation and citation standards).
